* t96linkmnf.a -- CONF68K test t96: F$Link of a module that is not there* reports E$MNF.** Claim (DOCS/claims.md): F$Link "causes OS-9 to search the module directory* for a module having a name, language, and type as given in the parameters",* and E$MNF is first in its own error list (F$Link, page 1 - 41). The* appendix defines 221 as the code for a request to link to a module that is* not found (page Error Codes - 6).** The name asked for is one no system can be carrying, and the type is 0 --* "any", per the same page's INPUT -- so nothing but absence can account for* the refusal: a system that answered E$MNF only for a type mismatch would* pass t78 and fail nothing, and this test would not tell them apart if it* named a type.** obs is the error code F$Link gave, or 0 if it claimed to find the module.*Prgrm set 1Objct set 1ReEnt set $80Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+ObjctAttr_Rev set (ReEnt<<8)+1FLink set $00*EXPECT set 221* psect t96linkmnf,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,2048,startstart moveq #0,d6 lea nosuch(pc),a0 moveq #0,d0 trap #0 dc.w FLink bcc.s t96v move.w d1,d6t96v cmpi.l #EXPECT,d6 beq.s ispass bra.s isfail* use verdict.i use report.i*nosuch dc.b "zqx_no_such_module",0idstr dc.b "RESULT t96 ",0passtr dc.b "PASS",0failstr dc.b "FAIL",0errstr dc.b "ERROR",0obsstr dc.b "  obs=",0expstr dc.b " exp=",0descstr dc.b "  F$Link of an absent module reports E$MNF",0 ends